{"task":"Proxy a site to a server already running, with a chosen offline behaviour","goals":"Let ldev serve a dev server directly, and make what happens when it stops a decision rather than an assumption","status":"Waiting","recorded_at":"2026-09-10T03:09:37Z","body":"A dev server is not a directory, so the wildcard server had no way to find one: every route\nin it resolves a hostname to files on disk, and the only escape was to hand a site its own\nCaddy and front that. For an app already listening on a port, that is a whole second server\nprocess to run something the wildcard one could proxy to directly.\n\n`ldev proxy <name> <port>` records the port in the site directory, and `ldev apply`\ngenerates the proxy block. No second process, portless URL, certificate as usual.\n\nThe port lives in the site's own .ldev-proxy rather than in ldev's config, for the same\nreason a standalone site's port lives in its own Caddyfile: it belongs to the site, and a\nsecond copy in a central file goes stale the first time somebody changes one and not the\nother.\n\nThe interesting case is not when the upstream is up. It is what the site does when the dev\nserver is stopped — which for a dev server is most of the time. Both answers are defensible\nand neither is obviously right, so it is a question rather than an assumption:\n\n  no   the proxy error. You always know the server is down.\n  yes  that site's last build. The URL keeps working, and can be arbitrarily stale while\n       looking perfectly alive.\n\nAsked during install, stored as PROXY_FALLBACK, carried forward on a re-install the same way\nthe ports now are, and overridable with --proxy-fallback for unattended runs. --defaults\nanswers no, because the honest failure is the safer default for somebody who did not choose.\n\nThe fallback is emitted as handle_errors, not handle_response: a dead upstream is a dial\nfailure, so reverse_proxy raises a 502 with no upstream reply to inspect, and\nhandle_response only ever sees responses that were actually sent. try_files {path}\n/index.html goes with it, since the thing being fallen back to is almost always a SPA build.\n\nThe fallback directory is stored absolute and is expected to be outside $SITES — build\noutput lives with the project, not with the site folder. Naming one while the setting is off\nis allowed and reported rather than silently ignored, since turning the setting on later\nshould not mean redoing the wiring.\n\ntest/proxy.test.sh runs a real Caddy against a real upstream rather than asserting on config\ntext: with the upstream serving it gets the upstream's response, and with the upstream\nkilled it gets the build. That is the assertion that would actually catch handle_errors\nbeing wrong, and the config-text checks around it would not.\n\nSet up on this machine: triaj.ldev now proxies to 5273 with its fallback recorded at\n~/Projects/triaj/apps/web/dist, which is what the Homebrew caddy on 443 was doing by hand.\n","parent_commit":"ae17bec1008e8c472aafefda000a4bb74a6e7a29","repo":"brave-cedar-fb33","pct":"70","tasks":[{"id":"proxy-cmd","content":"ldev proxy <name> <port> [--fallback <dir>], recording the port in the site's .ldev-proxy","status":"completed"},{"id":"proxy-gen","content":"Generate the reverse_proxy block from .ldev-proxy, no second server needed","status":"completed"},{"id":"fallback","content":"handle_errors serving the last build when the upstream is down","status":"completed"},{"id":"setting","content":"PROXY_FALLBACK asked at install time, carried forward on re-install, --proxy-fallback for unattended runs","status":"completed"},{"id":"list","content":"ldev list shows a proxied site and its port","status":"completed"},{"id":"tests","content":"test/proxy.test.sh — real Caddy, real upstream, upstream killed","status":"completed","created_at":"2026-09-10T01:14:35.832Z","status_at":"2026-09-10T01:14:35.832Z"},{"id":"docs","content":"README: the proxy section, the flags and config rows, the tests listing, the question count","status":"completed"},{"id":"dup","content":"Reconcile with claude/lucid-ember-7451, which rebuilt the free-port and root-Caddy detection independently","status":"pending","human_reason":"That branch's e8f7051 duplicates ed06eca (already on master) and b167a1c duplicates ae17bec (committed here), both in install.sh. Two sessions solved the same two problems in the same file, so landing that branch will conflict and one of the two implementations has to be chosen. Which to keep is Bryce's call; I could not identify the peer session reliably enough to message it without risking the wrong one."},{"id":"triaj-swap","content":"Retire sh.brew.caddy so ldev can own 443, now that triaj.ldev is a proxy site","status":"pending","human_reason":"Needs sudo and a terminal, which this session has neither of. triaj.ldev is now set up as a proxy site pointing at the same 5273 the Homebrew caddy proxies to, so the swap no longer breaks it — but stopping sh.brew.caddy and installing com.ldev.caddy is root-owned and briefly takes triaj.ldev and matsu.ldev offline. Re-run install.sh; it now finds sh.brew.caddy and offers to take it down."},{"id":"rehome-run","content":"Run ldev rehome against seasonal-drops","status":"pending","human_reason":"Rewrites live site data: 16,224 database rows plus WP_HOME/WP_SITEURL in wp-config.php. The command refuses to run without a terminal to confirm on. Do it after the swap, since the portless URL only works once the wildcard daemon owns 443. matsu and matsu-dev no longer have their own Caddyfiles, so only seasonal-drops still needs it.","created_at":"2026-09-09T19:22:30.481Z","status_at":"2026-09-09T19:22:30.481Z"}],"agent":"claude-code","changelog":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","code_quality":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","vulnerabilities":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","code_style_suggestions":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","reusability":"Unreviewed (agent unavailable).","system_compatibility":"Unreviewed (agent una